4. LOCAL MODIFICATIONS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

You MAY make Local Modifications to the Software for the purpose of
Operating it on systems you control. Local Modifications include:

  - patching bugs you encounter,
  - adjusting configuration values not exposed by .env or the panel UI,
  - adding integrations with third-party services you use,
  - translating the user interface into additional languages,
  - styling adjustments that do NOT violate Section 2,
  - performance tuning specific to your environment.

If you want a change to land upstream, you have exactly three permitted
channels:

  (a) **open an issue** on the official repository describing the bug,
      the request, or the proposal in detail;
  (b) **open a pull request** against the official repository carrying
      your change. By opening a pull request you grant the author an
      irrevocable, royalty-free, worldwide license to incorporate your
      contribution into the Software under this same License (or any
      future License the author chooses for the Software). The author
      may accept, modify, or decline the contribution at the author's
      sole discretion;
  (c) **contact the author directly** at the address in Section 14.

You MUST NOT publish a fork of the Software, whether on GitHub,
GitLab, Codeberg, Gitea, Bitbucket, SourceHut, a self-hosted Git
service, or any other code-hosting platform. Forking — in the sense
of creating a publicly visible copy of the repository under your own
account or organization, even one whose only purpose is to host a
proposed change — is Redistribution under Section 3 and is prohibited.
Use a private fork (GitHub allows this, and other platforms have
equivalents) or a local clone, push your branch there, and submit
your pull request from the private location.

You may use Local Modifications internally within an organization you
control, provided that such internal use does not constitute
Redistribution under Section 3 (i.e. the Software remains within the
legal control of a single User and is not handed to legally distinct
third parties).

You may NOT use Local Modifications as a vehicle to evade Section 2 or
Section 3. A modification whose purpose is to remove an Attribution Mark
or to enable Redistribution is itself a breach of this License.


